Description: hw/watchdog/wdt_imx2: Give the guest a grace period after a host stall
 Without -icount the virtual clock keeps running while the host does not
 run QEMU at all: the host sleeping, QEMU stopped by SIGSTOP, or a
 starved process. The Chumby's kernel arms the watchdog for 2.5 s and
 services it every 10 ms, but after any such stall longer than that the
 timeout has passed before the guest could run, and the machine resets.
 .
 Add a "stall-grace-ms" property, 0 (off) by default so other machines
 are unchanged. With it, a timeout does not act at once: the guest gets
 that much more time on the virtual clock, and a service in it cancels
 the reset. A hung guest is reset that much later. The pending grace
 timer is migrated in a subsection that only exists when the property
 is set.
 .
 With the i.MX21's 250 ms, stopping QEMU for 5, 10 and 30 s left the
 stock Chumby firmware running, and unloading chumby_sense1 (which stops
 the servicing) still reset the machine.
